#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168115 :tutorial/content "The **binormal vector** to a curve $C$ defined by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{B}(t) = \\mathbf{T}(t) \\times \\mathbf{N}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{T}$ and $\\mathbf{N}$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal vectors, respectively.\n\nThe diagram below shows the binormal vector to a curve at some point $P$ on the curve. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the curve\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: t \\right\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nAt [math]t=\\dfrac{5\\pi}6,[/math] the point on the curve, unit tangent, and principal normal are, respectively, given by\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) &= \\left\\langle 1, -\\sqrt3, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right\\rangle, \\\\[5pt]\n\n\\mathbf{T}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}\\left\\langle -\\sqrt3, -1, 1 \\right\\rangle, \\\\[5pt]\n\n\\mathbf{N}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left\\langle -1, \\sqrt3, 0 \\right\\rangle.\n\\end{align*}\n\nUsing the formula to calculate the binormal vector, we have\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align}\n\\mathbf{B}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\t&= \\mathbf{T}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) \\times \\mathbf{N}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}\\left\\langle -\\sqrt3, -1, 1 \\right\\rangle \\times \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left\\langle -1, \\sqrt3, 0 \\right\\rangle \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5}\\, \\begin{array}{|ccc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\sqrt3 & -1 & 1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & \\sqrt3 & 0\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5}\\left[ \\ \\begin{array}{|cc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & 1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\sqrt3 & 0\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\,\\mathbf i -\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\begin{array}{|cc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\sqrt3 & 1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & 0\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\,\\mathbf j +\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\begin{array}{|cc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\sqrt3 & -1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & \\sqrt3\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\,\\mathbf k \\ \\right] \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5} \\left\\langle (0-\\sqrt3), -(0-(-1)), (-3-1) \\right\\rangle \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5} \\left\\langle -\\sqrt3, -1, -4 \\right\\rangle.\n\\end{align}\n\\]\n\nBelow is a diagram of the curve [math]\\mathbf{r}(t),[/math] along with its tangent, normal, and binormal vectors at [math]t=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.[/math] Notice that [math]\\mathbf{T}[/math] and [math]\\mathbf{N}[/math] are both orthogonal to [math]\\mathbf{B}.[/math]\n\n![](images/95/95a892eb30b26d1255d29dcaaac552663443c00b2db6fe1e9168a77be061f2c0.png)\n\nSince $\\mathbf B = \\mathbf T\\times \\mathbf N,$ we can conclude that $\\mathbf B$ is perpendicular to both $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N,$ and it's pointing downward.\n\nAlso, since $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular *unit* vectors, it follows that $\\mathbf B$ is also a unit vector." :tutorial/id #uuid "eeccaac7-95df-5a69-8449-18e90f7ef33a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5544 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
